  <h1>Dog gut health: loose stools, gas, and what actually fixes them</h1>
  <p class="guide-lede">You can learn more about a dog's diet from the backyard than from the bag. If you're planning walks around your dog's stomach, this guide is for you — most gut trouble is a food problem wearing a medical costume.</p>

  <h2>What does a healthy dog stool look like?</h2>
  <p>Formed, moist but pick-up-able, chocolate brown, and consistent day to day. That's the whole test. Chronic softness, frequent color changes, mucus, or a dog who goes five times a day is telling you digestion isn't finishing the job — and the most common reason is that the food going in is heavy on fillers the gut was never going to use.</p>

  <h2>Why does my dog have so much gas?</h2>
  <p>Three usual suspects: gulped air from speed-eating, poorly digested carbohydrates and fillers fermenting where they shouldn't, and a protein that particular dog doesn't handle well. A slow-feeder bowl fixes the first. The other two live on the ingredient panel — high-filler kibble gives the gut more indigestible material to ferment, and that fermentation is the smell.</p>

  <h2>Why does my dog eat grass?</h2>
  <p>Occasional grazing is normal dog behavior and mostly harmless. Frantic, daily, gotta-get-outside grass eating — especially followed by vomiting — is worth treating as a signal of stomach upset and looking at the diet, not shrugging off.</p>

  <h2>What should I change first?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One protein, one food, no extras — for 4–6 weeks.</strong> Gut resets need a controlled experiment. Every table scrap restarts the clock.</li>
    <li><strong>Fewer, better ingredients.</strong> A short panel with a named protein first gives the gut less junk to argue with.</li>
    <li><strong>Probiotics and prebiotic fiber help — after the food is right.</strong> They're the finishing move, not the fix. A probiotic sprinkled on bad kibble is a mint after a bad meal.</li>
    <li><strong>Transition gradually.</strong> Even a great new food causes a week of upset if you switch overnight. Mix over 7–10 days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When is it a vet problem?</h2>
  <p>Blood in stool, black tarry stool, vomiting plus lethargy, a puppy or senior with diarrhea more than a day, or any dog refusing water — those are same-day vet calls, not diet experiments. Chronic issues that survive a genuine 6-week food reset also deserve a workup; parasites, pancreatitis, and IBD are real and diagnosable.</p>

